      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 The Author(s).",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "In biceps tenodesis for intra-articular tears, determining the distal extension of the lesions through the biceps groove is important in choosing the optimal tenodesis site."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To determine the optimal tenodesis site by analyzing the extension and delamination of an extra-articular lesion, or a \"hidden lesion,\" in the retrieved biceps after subpectoral biceps tenodesis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Case series; Level of evidence, 4."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 36 subpectoral tenodeses were performed, and the retrieved biceps were analyzed. The biceps lesions were divided into zones according to their location as follows: the proximal intra-articular (zone A), middle intragroove (zone B), and distal extra-articular portions (zone C); the lesions in zones B and C were called \"hidden lesions.\" The length and delamination depth of the biceps tears were examined, and the severity of the accompanying tenosynovitis and degeneration was assessed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Tears invaded zone B in all the cases and extended to zone C in 28 cases (77.8%). Tenosynovitis was observed along the tear in 28 cases (77.8%) and extended to zone C in 26 cases (72.2%). The mean tear length in the hidden lesions, including the tear and tenosynovitis, was 34.2 mm. Degenerative changes in the proximal intra-articular and middle intragroove portions were observed in all the cases and up to the distal extra-articular portion in 29 cases (80.6%)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "In approximately 80% of the intra-articular biceps tears evaluated in this study, a \"hidden lesion\" was observed going beyond the bicipital groove and extending to the distal extra-articular portion. Therefore, the subpectoral portion may be considered the optimal tenodesis site for the complete removal of all hidden biceps lesions."
          }
        ]
      },
